Local firewall policy goes here, in files ending in .nft.

/etc/nftables.conf loads /etc/nftables.d/*.nft last, in alphabetical order, so
rules placed here are appended to the chains it defines. Keeping local policy
here rather than editing /etc/nftables.conf means an appliance update to the
shipped policy will not discard it.

The input chain is default-deny, so an added rule only needs to accept:

    # /etc/nftables.d/10-snmp.nft
    add rule inet appliance input iif "eth0" udp dport 161 accept

To restrict a routing protocol to one interface, drop the broad rule from the
routing chain in /etc/nftables.conf and add a narrower one here.

Check a change before it takes effect, since a mistake here can close off SSH:

    nft --check --file /etc/nftables.conf
    systemctl reload nftables
